  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 8 (score 534.3, best so far): the oval finished 20 of 20 runs at 1.94 s with 0.25 line-loss events, so the oval is solved and has about 1 s of time margin. The s-bend finished 9 of 20 (progress 0.83), and the slalom still ends off track in all 20 runs (progress 0.17, 1.0 line-loss event per run). Steering oscillation is 0.2 to 0.35, so this is not jitter. The kd series is the clearest signal: kd 0.9 gave 382, kd 1.2 gave 451 and kd 1.5 gave 534, so each step still helps. Max error sits near the 0.06 m off-track limit with low RMS, which fits transient overshoot on bend entries. Decision: keep the geometry and every other controller value from iteration 8 and raise kd from 1.5 to 1.8 to add more damping. Expected result: s-bend progress and finish count rise, slalom progress rises above 0.17 with fewer line-loss events, and the oval holds near 0.95 or better with completion time near 2 s. Risk: higher kd amplifies sensor noise, and the gain may flatten, so the oval may lose some rms. Hypotheses for the next round: (H1) the slalom failure is still overshoot, so progress should keep rising with kd. (H2) If kd has reached diminishing returns (slalom progress flat, s-bend flat), turn-in speed is the limit, and base_speed should drop to about 1.1 (the oval would still finish under 3 s). (H3) If slalom line-loss stays near 1 per run, the array edge is the limit, and a 7-sensor array at ±0.045 m with graded weights should be tested. Measurements requested per track: completion, progress, completion_time, rms_error, max_error, line_loss_events, steering_oscillation, control_effort, controller_errors and noise_robustness, so that overshoot, edge loss and speed effects can be separated.",
